+# TrustMark-Q learned-carrier pilot
+
+## Outcome
+
+The preregistered validation gate selected global strength **1.0**. It achieved 4/4 clean and 20/24 non-clean exact validation recoveries, met every regional distortion threshold, and produced 0/2 valid unmarked decodes. Strength 1.5 had the same recovery counts but failed the frozen quality gate.
+
+Held-out confirmation was materially weaker: 19/28 exact, 9 abstentions, and no wrong valid messages. Clean, screenshot resize, JPEG Q55, and reference-assisted perspective/blur were 4/4; resize-plus-JPEG was 1/4, grayscale 2/4, and scanner bilevel 0/4. The validation hypothesis passed on its specified allocation, but the simulated robustness rate did not generalize to the confirmation sources. This is not evidence of physical recovery.
+
+This work reproduces Adobe TrustMark; it is integration evidence, not a NISHAN invention. No historical photo, `LEGACY_TRIAL_CAPTURE.jpeg`, new print, scan, camera capture, messaging app, GPU, or training run was used.
+
+## Frozen design and comparator
+
+- Official implementation: Adobe TrustMark Rust/ONNX at `2ecb73ad28d1a3f66ac9dc19e1b667711f14314f`, variant Q, BCH_5.
+- Sources: the unchanged common pack of two development, two validation, and two confirmation one-page PDFs, rendered RGB at 144 DPI (1190×1684 pixels).
+- Messages: two deterministic, independent, public synthetic 61-bit messages per source. Message generation is separate from registry-only receiver logic.
+- Comparison: strengths 1.0 and 1.5 used identical messages, validation sources, and transforms. Selection used validation only; confirmation consumed committed selection `da62826` and configuration `9047f3fd2ec457de8ee2e218fa8543fe40dbf8469d84da2324ac9f4e6c1d944f`.
+- Transforms: clean PNG, screenshot-style resize, JPEG Q55, resize-plus-JPEG Q55, grayscale, scanner-style autocontrast/bilevel, and simulated perspective/blur with the exact injected homography used for reference-assisted rectification.
+
+There is no matched original NISHAN-carrier baseline here: TrustMark carries 61 public data bits and has no collusion code, whereas the original profile has 52,500 symbols and a five-colluder threat model. Cross-profile accuracy or security superiority would be invalid.
+
+## Complete measured recovery
+
+| Allocation | Strengths | Exact | Wrong valid | Abstain | Unmarked valid |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| Development completeness (descriptive, run after confirmation) | 1.0 | 20/28 | 1 | 7 | 1/2 |
+| Validation matrix | 1.0 and 1.5 | 48/56 | 0 | 8 | 0/2 |
+| Validation selected-strength subset | 1.0 | 24/28 | 0 | 4 | 0/2 |
+| Confirmation | 1.0 | 19/28 | 0 | 9 | 0/2 |
+| All six sources at strength 1.0 | 1.0 | 63/84 | 1 | 20 | 1/6 |
+
+The one wrong-valid matrix output was an unregistered 61-bit BCH result from `dev_memo/B` after bilevel conversion (36 post-ECC message-bit differences). The unmarked `dev_table` also yielded a valid but unregistered 61-bit result (29 differences from its target). Exact registry lookup rejected both: **zero unissued identities were accepted**. These observations directly show why ECC success is not authentication and why valid-but-unregistered outputs must be retained in reporting.
+
+For issued matrix recoveries, every valid decoded output other than the one development false positive was the exact target (zero post-ECC message-bit errors). Raw pre-ECC bits/logits are unavailable through the official public Rust API and were not inferred.
+
+### Held-out confirmation by transform
+
+| Transform | Exact | Wrong valid | Abstain |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|
+| `clean_png` | 4/4 | 0 | 0 |
+| `screenshot_resize` | 4/4 | 0 | 0 |
+| `jpeg_q55` | 4/4 | 0 | 0 |
+| `resize_jpeg_q55` | 1/4 | 0 | 3 |
+| `grayscale` | 2/4 | 0 | 2 |
+| `scanner_bilevel` | 0/4 | 0 | 4 |
+| `perspective_blur_rectified` | 4/4 | 0 | 0 |
+
+All scanner-bilevel validation cases also abstained. Confirmation resize-plus-JPEG failures affected `test_short/B` and both `test_mixed` messages; grayscale failures affected both `test_mixed` messages.
+
+## Controls
+
+- Other-issued: a message-B image queried as target A recovered B exactly and was classified `other_issued`, never target A.
+- Wrong source/context: the donor message recovered, but the recipient-source registry rejected it as unregistered.
+- Donor residual transplant: copying the `validation_dense/A` pixel residual onto `validation_diagram` recovered the donor message exactly. The recipient registry rejected it. This demonstrates transplantability, not content authenticity, integrity, authorship, or culpability.
+- Unmarked: the official decoder returned a valid unregistered BCH message for 1/6 sources; the registry receiver accepted none.
+- Wrong key: not applicable because TrustMark is public and unkeyed; the frozen wrong-context registry control is not mislabeled as cryptographic key separation.
+
+## Distortion and readability
+
+At selected strength 1.0, validation clean global/ink/background PSNR ranges were 37.795–39.224 / 33.772–34.449 / 38.404–39.667 dB. Confirmation ranges were 37.271–44.075 / 33.840–34.629 / 37.599–44.518 dB, with 99th-percentile absolute channel error 7–13 levels. Strength 1.5 failed the quality gate because both `validation_dense` messages had global PSNR below 35 dB (34.596 and 34.746 dB).
+
+The fixed 1:1 crop panel was visually inspected. Text remained plainly readable, while faint background perturbation was discernible and stronger at 1.5. This is one inspection aid, not an invisibility claim or a human-subject quality study. Full per-case global, ink, and background PSNR/MAE values are preserved in JSON and CSV.
+
+Every marked output is raster-only. It loses selectable text, semantic structure, and accessibility features.
+
+## Payload and threat change
+
+TrustMark-Q consumes 100 model bits: 61 data, 35 BCH parity, and 4 version bits (two reserved and two schema bits). BCH_5 is configured for up to five correctable flips. The identifier and decoder are public; exact lookup in a frozen registry is only a bounded pilot mechanism.
+
+This experiment provides no cryptographic content binding, ledger guarantee, non-framing guarantee, human attribution, collusion resistance, or equivalence to NISHAN's original full-payload profile. The donor control shows that a valid watermark pattern can move between contents.
+
+## Runtime and resource evidence
+
+Runtime: Linux 7.2.4, Intel Core i5-8350U, Rust 1.85.0, `ort` 2.0.0-rc.8, Python 3.14.7, PyMuPDF 1.28.2, Pillow 12.3.0, NumPy 2.5.3, and OpenCV 5.0.0. ONNX intra-op, Rayon, and common numeric thread settings were 2, with inference pinned to CPUs 0–1.
+
+Across stages, unique encode calls took 0.506–0.587 s; decode calls took 0.243–0.365 s. Peak sampled child RSS was 350,516 KiB. Branch-local runtime/models/cache used 1,292,885,068 bytes; retained research results used about 1.1 MB and ignored work images about 56.8 MB, all below campaign limits. Timing uses Python monotonic time; RSS uses `/proc/<pid>/status` VmHWM/VmRSS sampled every 20 ms.
+
+## Print candidate and PDF verification
+
+The frozen print gate allowed one candidate: `results/print_candidate_test_mixed_A_strength_1p0.pdf`. It is a one-page A4 (595×842 pt) raster-only PDF at 144 DPI, visibly labelled as a TrustMark-Q research candidate with synthetic ID A, strength 1.0, and **NEW MATCHING PRINT ONLY**. The label was a named post-confirmation packaging variant that overwrote pixels in the source's misleading unmarked-reference footer. It did not re-embed, retune, or change the frozen payload; exact decoding was reverified after the edit.
+
+The labelled PNG and the PDF rendered back at 144 DPI both recovered the exact frozen 61-bit message. The deterministic final PDF SHA-256 is `cb92f99abf65de7174476328fe377a4be1a9ff2e8783a4f286a92538347ba944`; a repeat packaging run reproduced it byte-for-byte. Poppler QA confirmed one A4 page, no fonts/selectable text, and no clipping or overlap on the rendered page.
+
+A physical experiment requires a **new matching print**. The coordinator should place this page in a preregistered matched pack with an unmarked reference and original-profile control. It must not be tested on `LEGACY_TRIAL_CAPTURE.jpeg` or historical captures.
+
+## Measured result versus upstream claim
+
+Adobe's FAQ says printing is process-dependent and gives strength 1.5 as an example. This pilot did not test that claim. On these synthetic documents, 1.5 brought no validation recovery improvement over 1.0 and failed the preset visual-quality gate. Digital success under several transforms and failure under bilevel/combined resize-JPEG do not predict a printer-camera path.
+
+## Provenance and next action
+
+Official URLs, MIT attribution, observed model sizes/hashes, tensor conventions, payload/ECC audit, the narrow CLI/thread patch, source hashes, exact commands, per-case results, runtime details, and packaging verification are in `SOURCE_LICENSE_AUDIT.md`, `RUN_LOG.md`, `results/`, and `source_pack/manifest.json`.
+
+Next question for the coordinator/user: include the single gated candidate in a new matched physical pack, or stop this lead after the digital baseline? If physical testing is authorized, freeze printer, paper, scale, lighting, distance, angle, camera, crop/registration, and decision rules before capturing.
